diff --git a/Documentation/networking/mctp-i3c-target.rst b/Documentation/networking/mctp-i3c-target.rst
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..35267e1442d7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/networking/mctp-i3c-target.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+MCTP over I3C in target mode
+===========================
+
+The ``mctp-i3c-target`` driver connects a local I3C target to AF_MCTP. It
+creates an interface named ``mctpi3ctN``, where N is the I3C bus number,
+matching the ``mctpi3cN`` convention of the controller-side driver. The
+underlying target device is also reachable as ``parentdev N-target`` in
+``ip -d link show``. The interface has one physical
+upstream peer, the external I3C controller, and no link-layer address.
+All outgoing MCTP packets go to that controller, including packets whose
+destination EID is behind a bridge. No local MCTP neighbour entry is needed.
+
+This implementation uses the vendor kernel's I3C target API. Currently the
+AST2700 HCI driver implements the required atomic device-info operation.
+It does not change the I3C bus role or implement controller role handoff.
+
+Configuration
+-------------
+
+Enable ``CONFIG_MCTP``, ``CONFIG_I3C``, the AST2700 HCI driver, and
+``CONFIG_MCTP_TRANSPORT_I3C_TARGET``. The latter may also be a module,
+``mctp-i3c-target.ko``. It is enabled in ``aspeed_g7_defconfig``; an existing
+Yocto configuration may need an additional kernel config fragment.
+
+Select target mode and the network transport in the controller node::
+
+    &i3c4 {
+        initial-role = "target";
+        pid = <0x000007ec 0x06000000>;
+        dcr = /bits/ 8 <0xcc>;
+        mctp-controller;
+        status = "okay";
+    };
+
+The PID must be unique on the bus. The AST2700 DC-SCM base DTS contains
+this configuration. An overlay or derived DTS which removes
+``initial-role`` changes this controller back to primary mode and must
+not be used to test the target transport.
+
+``CONFIG_I3C_TARGET_MCTP`` may remain enabled. Its character-device driver
+declines target nodes with ``mctp-controller``, reserving them for this
+network driver irrespective of module load order. Without the property,
+the existing character-device behaviour is retained. With the property
+but without the new network driver, neither target MCTP interface binds.
+
+The external controller must assign a dynamic address and enable IBI
+before the target can transmit. This driver neither initiates DAA nor
+enables its own IBI. Target DCR is 0xcc; the IBI contains MDB 0xae, without
+the AST2600-specific extra MDB PEC workaround. Data packets have a software
+PEC covering the target address, direction bit, and MCTP packet bytes.
+
+Example setup with the ``mctp`` utility, using local EID 8 and controller
+EID 9 (substitute the actual interface name and assigned EIDs)::
+
+    mctp link show
+    mctp link set mctpi3ct0 up
+    mctp address add 8 dev mctpi3ct0
+    mctp route add 9 via mctpi3ct0
+
+Configure the corresponding route and neighbour on the external
+controller. A Linux controller running ``mctp-i3c`` uses the target's
+six-byte PID as its neighbour address. EID assignment and MCTP control
+commands remain the responsibility of userspace; the driver does not
+implement an MCTP control responder.
+
+Limits and error handling
+-------------------------
+
+The transmit MTU is fixed at 68 bytes: 64 bytes of MCTP payload plus its
+four-byte transport header. The core fragments larger messages. The
+additional PEC byte is not part of the MTU. Incoming packet lengths are
+bounded by the hardware receive buffer and current MWL. Probe requires
+MRL and MWL to support at least 69 bytes; transmit rechecks the current
+MRL on each packet.
+
+TX is serialized through a worker and uses the target pending-read API.
+It prepares data, sends an IBI, then waits for the controller's private
+read. IBI and pending-read waits are each bounded by one second. Missing
+DA, disabled IBI, inadequate MRL, and transport errors drop the packet and
+update network statistics. AF_MCTP send success means the packet was
+queued; it does not guarantee delivery to the remote endpoint.
+
+There is no transport retransmission queue in this first implementation.
+An IBI rejection or interrupted read is reported as a failed transfer;
+the application must retry its request as appropriate. Dynamic addresses
+are read from hardware for each RX/TX. An address change while a packet
+is already pending can invalidate its PEC; quiesce traffic during bus
+reconfiguration and retry requests afterwards. Polling-only operation,
+automatic role switching, and the AST2600 controller MDB workaround are
+not implemented.
+
+Validation on hardware
+----------------------
+
+* Verify the active DT still selects target mode, the I3C target device
+  binds to ``mctp-i3c-target``, and ``mctp link show`` lists its interface.
+* Have the external controller assign DA and enable IBI. Exchange MCTP
+  control or PLDM requests and responses with an AF_MCTP application.
+  Confirm the target sends MDB 0xae followed by a separate private read.
+* Exchange messages larger than 64 bytes to exercise core fragmentation
+  and reassembly. Check PEC in both directions with a bus trace.
+* Inject corrupt PEC, short packets, and oversized writes; verify drops
+  and statistics without memory corruption.
+* Disable IBI, reject an IBI, omit a private read, and terminate a read
+  early. Check that TX recovers for a subsequent valid transaction and
+  does not report failed transfers as successes.
+* Exercise RSTDAA and address reassignment, then resume traffic. Test
+  interface down/up and driver unbind/rebind with an outstanding TX.
+* Exercise both PIO and DMA paths on the relevant AST2700 revision.
+
+Compilation does not validate hardware timing, PEC register semantics,
+CCC handling, or DMA abort behaviour. Those checks require a board and
+the external controller firmware.
